1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,184
AllQuant: quantized inference kernels that run on AMD, NVIDIA and Intel GPUs.

2
00:00:05,184 --> 00:00:10,092
First, UltraQuant: one pass over a four bit KV cache, for speculative decoding.

3
00:00:10,092 --> 00:00:15,621
Then two papers we reproduced and ported to all three vendors, HyperQuant and PearlQuant.

4
00:00:15,621 --> 00:00:19,659
Along the way, what we measured, and where each one does not win.

5
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:24,407
Speculative decoding verifies K draft tokens in one step.

6
00:00:24,407 --> 00:00:30,652
A straightforward kernel handles each of the K queries separately, so each one reads the entire

7
00:00:30,652 --> 00:00:31,638
KV cache again.

8
00:00:31,638 --> 00:00:35,845
Decode is memory bound, so that costs close to K times the time.

9
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:38,799
The idea is to fuse them.

10
00:00:38,799 --> 00:00:45,817
All K queries, and the query heads that share a key value head, are stacked into one tile.

11
00:00:45,817 --> 00:00:51,509
Each page of the cache is read once, and all K outputs come out together.

12
00:00:51,509 --> 00:00:53,224
On the left, K passes.

13
00:00:53,224 --> 00:00:54,628
On the right, one.

14
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:57,988
The cache is stored in four bits.

15
00:00:57,988 --> 00:01:04,633
Each value is an FP4 E2M1 float, and every thirty two values share one eight bit power of two

16
00:01:04,633 --> 00:01:06,062
scale, called UE8M0.

17
00:01:06,062 --> 00:01:12,636
At head dimension one twenty eight that is 68 bytes per head per token, against 256 for half

18
00:01:12,636 --> 00:01:14,494
precision and 128 for fp8.

19
00:01:14,494 --> 00:01:17,852
That is 3.76 times smaller than half precision.

20
00:01:18,850 --> 00:01:21,927
Inside the kernel, the queries form one tile.

21
00:01:21,927 --> 00:01:28,354
FP4 pages are expanded to bf16 in registers, each query gets its own causal mask, and split KV

22
00:01:28,354 --> 00:01:32,183
partial results are merged with a log sum exp reduction.

23
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:39,512
The same operation runs on three vendors: a Triton kernel on AMD MI300X, native CUDA on five

24
00:01:39,512 --> 00:01:43,780
NVIDIA GPUs, and SYCL with XMX and ESIMD on an Intel Arc B390.

25
00:01:43,780 --> 00:01:49,906
All three read the same cache layout, a pure permute of the upstream one, and are checked

26
00:01:49,906 --> 00:01:55,894
against the same CPU reference vectors: 8,228 CUDA launches and 36 SYCL runs, with zero

27
00:01:55,894 --> 00:01:56,513
failures.

28
00:01:57,510 --> 00:02:02,907
Time for one attention layer, batch eight, thirty two thousand tokens of context.

29
00:02:02,907 --> 00:02:06,572
Going from one speculative token to eight costs little.

30
00:02:06,572 --> 00:02:09,838
On MI300X it goes from 0.36 to 0.54 milliseconds.

31
00:02:09,838 --> 00:02:11,637
On H100, from 0.16 to 0.22.

32
00:02:11,637 --> 00:02:13,436
On B300, from 0.14 to 0.31.

33
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:21,459
Against the same kernel with KV sharing off, the fused kernel at K equals eight is 4.1 times

34
00:02:21,459 --> 00:02:25,960
faster on MI300X, 5.6 times on H100, and 3.3 times on B300.

35
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:31,682
Across all six GPUs, the gain at K equals eight runs from 2.5 to 5.6 times.

36
00:02:31,682 --> 00:02:34,810
At K equals one there is nothing to fuse.

37
00:02:35,810 --> 00:02:38,150
How does it compare with other schemes?

38
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:43,851
We ported TurboQuant, NVFP4, FP8, KIVI, QServe, KVQuant, SpectralQuant, and the Planar, Iso and

39
00:02:43,851 --> 00:02:46,492
Rotor quantisers to the same MI300X harness.

40
00:02:46,492 --> 00:02:52,013
Speed is horizontal, perplexity increase on Qwen three eight B is vertical, so lower left is

41
00:02:52,013 --> 00:02:52,433
better.

42
00:02:52,433 --> 00:02:57,593
UltraQuant beats TurboQuant, SpectralQuant and the three bit rotation methods on both.

43
00:02:57,593 --> 00:02:59,514
But it is not the most accurate.

44
00:03:00,510 --> 00:03:03,446
On accuracy, UltraQuant is not the leader.

45
00:03:03,446 --> 00:03:09,319
QServe int four at the same sixty eight bytes, NVFP4, FP8 and KIVI four all beat it.

46
00:03:09,319 --> 00:03:15,471
NVFP4, for four more bytes, cuts the Qwen three perplexity increase from 0.562 to 0.081.

47
00:03:15,471 --> 00:03:17,917
FP8 is also faster at K equals one.

48
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:21,235
On B300, a vendor kernel is faster.

49
00:03:21,235 --> 00:03:27,452
FlashInfer's NVFP4 decode, which also shares the read across queries, takes 0.066 milliseconds

50
00:03:27,452 --> 00:03:30,097
at K equals one, against 0.139 for ours.

51
00:03:30,097 --> 00:03:32,809
That is 1.8 to 3.3 times faster across K.

52
00:03:32,809 --> 00:03:35,587
We do not beat vendor tuned kernels there.

53
00:03:36,590 --> 00:03:37,749
In a real server?

54
00:03:37,749 --> 00:03:44,090
We ran vLLM's own native four bit backend, not our kernel, with Qwen three eight B on MI300X.

55
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:50,432
KV capacity rises from 1.09 million tokens to 3.16 million, which is 2.89 times, because vLLM

56
00:03:50,432 --> 00:03:52,886
keeps four layers at full precision.

57
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:55,159
The costs are real.

58
00:03:55,159 --> 00:03:58,564
Question answering F one drops from 0.655 to 0.618.

59
00:03:58,564 --> 00:04:04,640
Greedy output diverges from bf sixteen after a median of 10 tokens, earlier than fp8 at 24.

60
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:08,579
Plain decode is slower: 767 tokens per second against 1052.

61
00:04:08,579 --> 00:04:13,520
In an oversubscribed agent test FP4 wins, 59 against 48 tokens per second.

62
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:17,168
We also reproduce and port other papers.

63
00:04:17,168 --> 00:04:23,390
First, HyperQuant: a Hadamard rotation, the E8 lattice and Rice coding, for weights and the KV

64
00:04:23,390 --> 00:04:23,788
cache.

65
00:04:23,788 --> 00:04:29,812
On an H100 its perplexity tables reproduce: our bf16 baseline is 7.1597 against the paper's

66
00:04:29,812 --> 00:04:32,658
7.1606, and every E8 row lands within 0.02.

67
00:04:32,658 --> 00:04:35,902
We then ported every CUDA kernel to HIP and SYCL.

68
00:04:35,902 --> 00:04:41,992
All 6 backends pass the same 84 of 86 shared checks; the two failures are upstream bugs, and

69
00:04:41,992 --> 00:04:43,779
every port reproduces them.

70
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:45,806
Speed is mixed.

71
00:04:45,806 --> 00:04:52,238
On MI300X the stand alone Rice decoders run at 0.59 to 1.08 times the H100 time, but the fused

72
00:04:52,238 --> 00:04:57,644
matrix and attention kernels are one and a half to two and a half times slower.

73
00:04:57,644 --> 00:04:59,834
These kernels are latency bound.

74
00:04:59,834 --> 00:05:06,061
And the paper's throughput table does not reproduce: Rice weights decode at 10.8 tokens per

75
00:05:06,061 --> 00:05:08,113
second, against 75.6 for bf16.

76
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:13,629
Second, PearlQuant: an E8 lattice KV cache codec from Pearl Research.

77
00:05:13,629 --> 00:05:17,427
They published no code, so this is our own reconstruction.

78
00:05:17,427 --> 00:05:23,517
The rate distortion shape reproduces: 0.38 wasted bits at two point seven five bits per value

79
00:05:23,517 --> 00:05:24,892
against Pearl's 0.39.

80
00:05:24,892 --> 00:05:31,048
On Qwen three eight B, our KL divergence is about 1.7 to 2.2 times what Pearl reports on their

81
00:05:31,048 --> 00:05:31,703
own model.

82
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:39,376
Our fused decode attention passes all 96 shared checks on 7 targets, across CUDA, HIP and SYCL.

83
00:05:39,376 --> 00:05:45,982
But it is ALU bound: 1.5 to 3.5 times slower than bf16 attention, while storing a fifth of the

84
00:05:45,982 --> 00:05:46,404
bytes.

85
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:48,303
Honest caveats.

86
00:05:48,303 --> 00:05:51,313
Most kernel numbers are synthetic microbenchmarks.

87
00:05:51,313 --> 00:05:57,031
The serving results come from vLLM's native backend, not our kernel, as single runs on a shared

88
00:05:57,031 --> 00:05:57,272
GPU.

89
00:05:57,272 --> 00:06:02,389
MI300X and Arc have no native FP4 matrix instructions, so FP4 is decoded in software.

90
00:06:02,389 --> 00:06:05,098
And on B300, vendor NVFP4 kernels are faster.

91
00:06:05,098 --> 00:06:10,756
For the ports, HyperQuant's throughput claims do not reproduce, and our PearlQuant style codec

92
00:06:10,756 --> 00:06:14,067
is a reconstruction that is slower than bf16 attention.

93
00:06:15,070 --> 00:06:16,457
So, is it the best?

94
00:06:16,457 --> 00:06:16,676
No.

95
00:06:16,676 --> 00:06:18,283
Here is where it wins.

96
00:06:18,283 --> 00:06:22,518
One validated implementation across AMD, NVIDIA and Intel.

97
00:06:22,518 --> 00:06:29,381
A fused K query pass that is 2.5 to 5.6 times faster than the same kernel with KV sharing off.

98
00:06:29,381 --> 00:06:32,521
And 3.76 times more KV tokens per gigabyte.

99
00:06:32,521 --> 00:06:39,092
In the public sources we checked, we found no public CUDA or SYCL port of this layout with

100
00:06:39,092 --> 00:06:40,626
fused K query verify.

101
00:06:41,630 --> 00:06:46,445
The code, the results, and the blog post are all in the repo.

